							<div class="exit-item"><strong>2</strong><p>Why do we subtract <span class="mathjax" data-tex="x"></span> from both sides, not only one side?</p></div>
							<div class="exit-item"><strong>3</strong><p>Check whether <span class="mathjax" data-tex="x = 5"></span> is correct for <span class="mathjax" data-tex="4x - 1 = 2x + 9"></span>.</p></div>
							<div class="exit-item"><strong>4</strong><p>Write one equation that has <span class="mathjax" data-tex="x"></span> on both sides.</p></div>
						</div>
						<button type="button" class="primary-button" data-toggle-target="exit-answers">Show sample answers</button>
						<div id="exit-answers" class="sample-answer hidden-answer">
							<p><strong>1.</strong> <span class="mathjax" data-tex="x = 5"></span></p>
							<p><strong>2.</strong> Because an equation must stay balanced; whatever we do to one side, we do to the other side.</p>
							<p><strong>3.</strong> <span class="mathjax" data-tex="4(5)-1=19"></span> and <span class="mathjax" data-tex="2(5)+9=19"></span>, so it is correct.</p>
							<p><strong>4.</strong> Example: <span class="mathjax" data-tex="2x + 3 = x + 8"></span>.</p>
